 &nbsp; 
 
 Cleaning out the notebook as we kick off the first full week of Q4... 

 🔨 Regulatory revelation: The SEC last week called for increased rigor when it comes to private asset valuations, with a particular focus on private credit.

 Consider this something of a regulatory compass, showing where the SEC is paying closest attention. Most notable, the SEC reminded private credit managers that they are required to make fair value measurements on a timely basis — with macro events to be considered — even if not contractually obligated to provide reports in the same time frame to investors. It also emphasized that valuation techniques and inputs must be clearly disclosed, adding that "boilerplate" language may not be sufficient. 💼 VC-land: Notable Capital is out raising its 10th fund, but without managing partner Jeff Richards on the Form D . Fellow managing partners Glenn Solomon and Hans Tung are listed.

 Richards declined to discuss fundraising, natch, but tells me via text: "I remain 100% active and engaged; just decided not to make new investments from the next fund. It gives our younger investors like Dan [Cahana] and Chelcie [Taylor] room to run ... I currently sit on 14 boards, five of which are &gt;$100m ARR, so quite busy ... It's an incredible time to be in venture in Silicon Valley." Notable is the U.S. venture group that once was known as GGV Capital, before splitting with its China arm due to geopolitical tensions. 💰 More VC-land: Chad Byers is raising a new eponymous venture fund, per an SEC filing , but tells me that he isn't leaving Susa Ventures — which he co-founded. He declined to comment further.

 Byers Capital Group 1 lists the same office address as Susa, but doesn't list its other general partners. 🎵 Coming attractions: Don't be surprised to see Shamrock Capital in market soon with its second debt opportunities fund.

 The LA-based firm, which focuses on media and entertainment deals, is said to have fully deployed its debut $191 million debt fund — quietly closing 10 loans, only two of which have been publicly announced (Welsh soccer club Swansea City and Roblox game developer Voldex). It can recycle via that fund's realizations, and has dry powder for equity investments out of its flagship and content funds, but there's growing demand in areas like music and sports for asset-backed loans — particularly among content owners who don't want to dilute their ownership stakes. 🤖 Robotic rupture: There's a disagreement among venture capitalists when it comes to funding physical AI, which was explored a bit last week during my on-stage interview with Bain Capital Ventures partner Ajay Agarwal. 

 Some are plugging big money into robot "brain" developers, believing that the trained models can be plugged into a variety of different hardware. The physics, they argue, remain constant, and hardware is often where venture dollars go to die. Agarwal is on the other side, perhaps based on his experience backing Kiva Systems — which was acquired by Amazon and became the foundation for its warehouse robotics system. He believes that the winners will be full-stack, due to the need for sector specialization. ✨ SV-to-DC: Andreessen Horowitz spurred a lot of Silicon Valley's shift toward Republicans in the 2024 elections, and now has one of its top alumni on President Trump's new AI task force (I'm sorry, his new "Super Intelligence Force").

 Scott Kupor was one of a16z's first employees, spending 16 years as managing partner before leaving to join Trump 2.0 as head of the Office of Personnel Management (a role he'll keep). The group is being chaired by DNI Jay Clayton , and also includes FTC chair Andrew Ferguson and Uber vet and current Pentagon CTO Emil Michael. 
 
